CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is an isometric schematic of a deep network drawn as a tall rectangular box standing on end between two labeled faces. The word Data sits below the bottom face and the word Embedding sits above the top face. Inside, three light gray parallelogram planes are stacked at even heights to stand for intermediate layers, with the box's outer edges in solid black and the interior planes outlined in a lighter gray. Five dotted curves thread up through the stack, each starting at a filled round dot on or near the lower planes and ending in an arrowhead just short of the top face, so a single datapoint can be traced through every layer. A long vertical arrow at the far left points upward beside the rotated text Representation learning, and a matching arrow at the far right points downward beside Generative modeling. The two opposed arrows carry the encoding: the same stack of layers is read bottom-up in one case and top-down in the other. The picture sets up the claim that representation learning and generative modeling are two readings of one architecture, and invites the question of what actually separates data from an embedding.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Tall rectangular box (square footprint, standing on end) drawn in solid black wireframe edges only — no shaded faces
  - Bottom square face of the box (the 'Data' space), rendered as a diamond in isometric view
  - Top square face of the box (the 'Embedding' space), rendered as a diamond
  - Four vertical corner edges of the box, solid black
  - Three light-gray semi-transparent square planes stacked at evenly spaced heights inside the box, representing intermediate layers, each outlined in medium gray
  - Five dotted curves threading upward through the stack, each gently S-curved, roughly vertical, distributed across the footprint
  - Five filled round dots marking the start of each curve near/below the lowest plane (some black on the bottom face, some gray sitting on the lowest plane)
  - Five arrowheads terminating each dotted curve just below the top face
  - Long vertical arrow at far left pointing UP, solid black with a single arrowhead at the top
  - Rotated vertical text label 'Representation learning' beside the left arrow
  - Long vertical arrow at far right pointing DOWN, solid black with a single arrowhead at the bottom
  - Rotated vertical text label 'Generative modeling' beside the right arrow
  - Text label 'Embedding' centered above the top face
  - Text label 'Data' centered below the bottom face

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World space: box footprint 4 × 4 units in the XZ plane (X = screen-right-ish, Z = receding diagonal), height 8 units in Y, centered at origin so bottom face y = -4 and top face y = +4. Three interior layer planes are 4 × 4 squares parallel to the faces, at y = -1.6, y = 0.0, y = +1.6 (evenly spaced, occupying only the middle ~40% of the box height — there is a visibly larger empty gap above the top plane and below the bottom plane, as in the source). Box drawn as wireframe only: 12 edges, solid black #000000, ~2.5px, no faces filled. Interior planes: fill #e6e6e6 at ~0.55 opacity (double-overlap regions read darker, ~#d0d0d0), outline #6b6b6b ~1.5px. Five dotted curves: color #2b2b2b to #555555, dot/dash pattern, line width ~2. Curve start dots (radius ~0.09): three near the bottom face at (x,z) ≈ (-1.2,-0.4) y=-3.0, (-0.1,-1.0) y=-3.4, (+1.4,-0.8) y=-3.3, colored black #111111; two gray dots #6a6a6a resting on the lowest plane at (x,z) ≈ (-0.4,+0.9) y=-1.6 and (+0.9,+0.5) y=-1.6. Each curve rises with a gentle horizontal S-wobble of ±0.4 units and ends with a cone arrowhead at y ≈ +3.4, just below the top face, tips fanning slightly outward. Left vertical arrow: at x ≈ -4.6, spanning y = -4 to +4, solid black, arrowhead at TOP; label 'Representation learning' rotated 90° CCW, placed just outside it. Right vertical arrow: at x ≈ +4.6, spanning the same y range, arrowhead at BOTTOM; label 'Generative modeling' rotated 90° CW (reading bottom-to-top in the source), placed just outside it. 'Embedding' centered above the top vertex at y ≈ +5.6; 'Data' centered below the bottom vertex at y ≈ -5.6. All text black serif. Background white. When the direction toggle is set to generative modeling, reverse each dotted curve's arrowhead to the lower end and dim the left arrow/label to ~30% gray while the right arrow/label is full black (and vice versa). For layerStep highlighting, the active plane gets outline #111111 at 2.5px and fill opacity 0.8, and the five point markers are drawn at that plane's height at their per-layer (x,z) positions (positions interpolate smoothly along the same dotted curves).

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
